      "_comment": "Development evidence in the JD, in two tiers. 'signals' = all development terms; 'strong_signals' = terms that mean building software (frameworks, services, APIs, distributed/backend, app languages like Java/Go/TS, CUDA/vLLM, low-latency). Python, C/C++/C#, SQL, 'algorithm', 'unit test' are weak: engineers in every field use them for scripting. Strict check (generic / ai titles without 'developer' or 'programmer'): at least min_signals development terms, at least one strong term, and more development than penalty terms (dominance, not subtraction: one stray word can't cancel the evidence). Lenient check (MTS, Programmer Analyst, and generic titles that say developer/programmer): rejected only when penalty terms outnumber development terms. Regression set: tests/fixtures/golden_roles.json + tests/test_golden.py. Bare programming-language names count as ONE term (a requirements line listing 'C, C++, Java, Python' is one fact, not six), like SQL/HTML/CSS and bus protocols. Embedded/firmware words are NOT development evidence here: they belong to the embedded track, and counting them on both sides made the track's dominance test impossible to meet (review, 15 Nov).",
